+{
+  "name": "Genetics Laboratory",
+  "category": "forestry:binnie_genetics",
+  "icon": "binniegenetics:isolator",
+  "sortnum": 0,
+  "pages": [
+    {
+      "type": "patchouli:text",
+      "text": "(Temporary LLM text) The core workflow is: isolate an allele from an organism, sequence the isolated material, turn known genes into charged serum material, and apply those genes to another compatible organism. The Analyser and Genepool support the same laboratory by revealing genomes and producing Raw DNA.",
+      "title": "Laboratory workflow"
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "patchouli:spotlight",
+      "item": "binniegenetics:empty_sequencer",
+      "text": "(Temporary LLM text) An Empty Sequencer is consumed by the Isolator when it captures a sampled allele. The resulting genetic material is not useful for gene application until it has been sequenced.",
+      "title": "Sequence vials"
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "patchouli:spotlight",
+      "item": "binniegenetics:enzyme",
+      "text": "(Temporary LLM text) Laboratory reagents carry charges and are consumed gradually by compatible machines. The Isolator and Genepool use Enzyme; the Sequencer uses Fluorescent Dye; and the Analyser uses DNA Dye.",
+      "title": "Charged reagents"
+    }
+  ]
+}
